[{"id":"30021","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Which of the following is the use of bleaching powder?</span></p>","option_1":"It is used in soda-acid fire extinguishers.","option_2":"As an oxidising agent in many chemical industries.","option_3":"It is used for removing permanent hardness of water.","option_4":"It is used in the manufacture of sodium compounds such as borax.","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - As an oxidising agent in many chemical industries.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Bleaching powder (calcium oxychloride, CaOCl2) is widely used as an oxidising agent in many chemical industries because it readily releases chlorine, which acts as a strong oxidiser. It is also used for bleaching textiles and paper pulp, and for disinfecting drinking water.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Soda-acid fire extinguishers use sodium bicarbonate and sulphuric acid (not bleaching powder); permanent hardness of water is removed using methods like Clark's process or ion exchange resins; and sodium compounds like borax are manufactured through separate processes. Chemistry of compounds and their industrial uses is a common SSC General Science topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30022","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>How many banks in India were nationalised in the year 1969?</span></p>","option_1":"28","option_2":"14","option_3":"9","option_4":"35","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 14.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>On 19 July 1969, the Government of India nationalised 14 major commercial banks (each having deposits of over Rs 50 crore) under the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Act, 1969, as part of the policy to extend banking to rural areas and priority sectors.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>A second round of nationalisation in 1980 covered 6 more banks. Bank nationalisation is a very commonly tested SSC Economy/History fact.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30023","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Which of the following is an example of peninsular river?</span></p>","option_1":"Mahanadi","option_2":"Brahmaputra","option_3":"Ganga","option_4":"Indus","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Mahanadi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Mahanadi is a classic example of a peninsular river, originating in the Chhattisgarh plains of the Deccan Plateau and flowing through a fixed, rain-fed course without any glacial input.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Ganga, Brahmaputra, and Indus are all Himalayan rivers, fed by snow and glacial melt, originating in the Himalayas. Peninsular rivers (e.g., Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri) versus Himalayan rivers is a fundamental SSC Geography classification.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30024","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Which of the following statement is correct regarding westerlies?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>I. It is found between sub-tropical high pressure belt and sub-polar low pressure belt.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>II. It is a local wind.</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Only I.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Westerlies are permanent planetary winds that blow from the sub-tropical high-pressure belt (around 30 degrees latitude) towards the sub-polar low-pressure belt (around 60 degrees latitude) in both hemispheres, hence Statement I is correct.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>They are not a local wind - local winds are confined to smaller regions caused by local temperature/pressure differences (e.g., land and sea breezes), whereas westerlies are global/planetary wind systems, making Statement II incorrect. Global wind belts are a standard SSC Geography topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30025","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Which is the main coordinating centre of the human body?</span></p>","option_1":"Spleen","option_2":"Brain","option_3":"Liver","option_4":"Lung","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Brain.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The brain is the main coordinating centre of the human body, being the central organ of the nervous system that receives sensory information, processes it, and sends out motor commands, coordinating and regulating all bodily activities.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The spleen filters blood, the liver performs metabolic/detoxification functions, and the lungs perform gas exchange - none of which serve a coordinating role. This is a basic Human Physiology concept in SSC General Science.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30026","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Which of the following inspired Gautam Buddha to renounce worldly life?</span></p>","option_1":"A teacher who explained suffering","option_2":"An ascetic, who appeared to be happy and at peace.","option_3":"A conversation with a king","option_4":"Dreams and visions","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - An ascetic, who appeared to be happy and at peace.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>According to Buddhist tradition, Prince Siddhartha was profoundly moved by the 'Four Sights' encountered outside his palace: an old man, a sick man, a corpse, and finally a wandering ascetic who, despite having no possessions, appeared serene and at peace.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>This ascetic's peaceful demeanour amid renunciation inspired Siddhartha to leave his royal life in search of a solution to human suffering, marking the beginning of the 'Great Renunciation'. This is a key event in Buddhist history tested in SSC Ancient History/Culture.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30027","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The dance form 'Sammi' originates from which of the following Indian states?</span></p>","option_1":"Jharkhand","option_2":"Maharashtra","option_3":"Assam","option_4":"Punjab","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Punjab.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>'Sammi' is a traditional folk dance of Punjab, particularly popular in the Hoshiarpur region, performed by women, often accompanying folk songs that reflect themes of love, longing, and daily rural life.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Folk dances of Indian states are a common SSC Art &amp; Culture topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30028","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The dance form 'Ajhi Lhamu', performed by the Monpa tribe, belongs to which Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Arunachal Pradesh","option_2":"Andhra Pradesh","option_3":"Madhya Pradesh","option_4":"Himanchal Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Arunachal Pradesh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>'Ajhi Lhamu' is a traditional dance performed by the Monpa tribe of Arunachal Pradesh, often associated with Buddhist festivals and monastic traditions prevalent among the Monpa community, who predominantly inhabit the Tawang and West Kameng districts.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Folk dances of northeastern tribal communities are a recurring SSC Culture topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30029","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>'The Feast of St. Francis Xavier' is celebrated in Goa in which month?</span></p>","option_1":"January","option_2":"August","option_3":"December","option_4":"May","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - December.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Feast of St. Francis Xavier is celebrated in Goa on December 3 every year, commemorating the patron saint of Goa, whose mortal remains are preserved at the Basilica of Bom Jesus in Old Goa.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>This is a significant religious/cultural festival tested in SSC General Awareness (Festivals of India).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30030","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Which of the following is an example of non-contact force?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>I. Magnetic force</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>II. Gravitational force</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Both magnetic force and gravitational force are non-contact forces, meaning they act on objects without any physical contact - magnetic force acts between magnetic materials/charges at a distance, and gravitational force acts between any two masses at a distance.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Hence both statements are correct. Contact versus non-contact forces (e.g., friction and normal force being contact forces) are a standard SSC Physics topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30031","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>In which year was the Treaty of Salbai signed between the British and the Marathas?</span></p>","option_1":"1782","option_2":"1792","option_3":"1803","option_4":"1817","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 1782.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Treaty of Salbai was signed in 1782 between the British East India Company and the Marathas, ending the First Anglo-Maratha War and restoring the status quo prevailing before the war.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>It established peace between the two powers for about two decades, until the outbreak of the Second Anglo-Maratha War in 1803. Anglo-Maratha wars and treaties are a common SSC Modern History topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30032","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>In the context of Chandrayaan-III mission, the ILSA payload was used to ______.</span></p>","option_1":"detect moonquakes","option_2":"study Earth's spectral and polarimetric properties from lunar orbit","option_3":"measure thermal conductivity from lunar surface","option_4":"study the plasma density on lunar surface","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - detect moonquakes.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>ILSA (Instrument for Lunar Seismic Activity), one of the payloads on the Vikram lander of Chandrayaan-3, was designed to detect and measure moonquakes (seismic activity on the Moon) and study characteristics of the lunar crust and mantle.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>ChaSTE measured thermal conductivity, RAMBHA-LP studied plasma density, and SHAPE (aboard the propulsion module) studied Earth's spectral/polarimetric properties - these were other, separate payloads of the mission. Chandrayaan-3 payload details are a frequently tested SSC Science &amp; Technology/current-affairs topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30033","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The population of India according to their economic status is generally divided into ______ groups.</span></p>","option_1":"3","option_2":"6","option_3":"7","option_4":"5","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 3.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The population of India, according to economic status, is generally divided into three broad groups: Above Poverty Line (APL), Below Poverty Line (BPL), and the ultra-poor/destitute (Antyodaya) category.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>This classification is used for targeted welfare schemes and public distribution system allocations. This is a standard SSC Economy/Welfare Schemes topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30034","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>\"Vikram-S\" India's first ever private launch vehicle has been developed by ______.</span></p>","option_1":"Skyroot Aerospace","option_2":"Agnikul Cosmos","option_3":"Bellatrix Aerospace","option_4":"Pixxel","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Skyroot Aerospace.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>\"Vikram-S\" was developed by Skyroot Aerospace, a Hyderabad-based private space-tech startup, and became India's first privately-developed rocket to be launched into space, under Mission 'Prarambh' from Sriharikota in November 2022.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Agnikul Cosmos, Bellatrix Aerospace, and Pixxel are other Indian private space-tech companies but were not behind Vikram-S. Recent developments in India's private space industry are a common current-affairs topic in SSC exams.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30035","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>'Padavali Kirtans', sung in devotional gatherings, are traditionally associated with which Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Haryana","option_2":"West Bengal","option_3":"Karnataka","option_4":"Telangana","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - West Bengal.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>'Padavali Kirtan' is a devotional musical tradition of West Bengal, rooted in Vaishnavism and centred on singing verses (padavali) composed in praise of Radha-Krishna.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>It is often performed in devotional gatherings (kirtan sessions) associated with the Bhakti movement popularised by Chaitanya Mahaprabhu. Regional devotional music traditions are a common SSC Art &amp; Culture topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30036","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Census Act, ______ and the Census Rules, 1990 provide the legal framework for conduct of Census.</span></p>","option_1":"1948","option_2":"1958","option_3":"1951","option_4":"1955","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 1948.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Census Act, 1948, along with the Census Rules, 1990, provides the legal framework governing the conduct of the Census of India, including provisions for confidentiality of individual data and penalties for non-compliance.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>This is a key legislative fact tested under SSC Polity/Census-related General Awareness.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30037","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Which country launched the Chamran-1 satellite in September 2024?</span></p>","option_1":"Israel","option_2":"India","option_3":"China","option_4":"Iran","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Iran.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Iran launched the Chamran-1 satellite in September 2024, aimed at testing technologies for satellite positioning and stabilisation, as part of its ongoing efforts to expand its indigenous space programme.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>This is a current-affairs fact on international space developments frequently tested in SSC exams.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30038","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The successful test of the indigenously developed 'VLSRSAM' by DRDO and the Indian Navy in March 2025 was conducted off the coast of which state?</span></p>","option_1":"Odisha","option_2":"Gujarat","option_3":"Kerala","option_4":"Tamil Nadu","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Odisha.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Defence Research and Development Organisation (DRDO), along with the Indian Navy, successfully tested the indigenously developed Vertical Launch Short Range Surface to Air Missile (VLSRSAM) off the coast of Odisha in March 2025.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>This strengthened India's naval air-defence capabilities against aerial threats at short ranges. Defence-related current affairs (DRDO missile tests) are a regularly tested SSC topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30039","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Which of the following statements is correct regarding Cobalt?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>I. Jharkhand has cobalt deposits.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>II. Cobalt is used in rechargeable batteries.</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Jharkhand does have cobalt deposits (along with states like Odisha and Nagaland, which also have notable reserves), and cobalt is indeed a critical component of lithium-ion rechargeable batteries, widely used in electric vehicles, smartphones, and laptops.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Hence both statements are correct. Mineral resources and their industrial applications (critical minerals) are an increasingly tested SSC Geography/current-affairs topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30040","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>In which year, was the first National Judo Championship held in India?</span></p>","option_1":"1966","option_2":"1988","option_3":"1976","option_4":"1956","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 1966.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The first National Judo Championship in India was held in 1966, marking the formal introduction and organisation of competitive judo at the national level in the country.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Sports history and 'firsts' in Indian sporting events are a standard SSC static GK topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30041","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>What is the name of the autobiography of actor Prem Chopra?</span></p>","option_1":"Prem Katha","option_2":"Bollywood Diary of Prem Chopra","option_3":"Life and Times of Prem Chopra","option_4":"Prem Naam Hai Mera, Prem Chopra","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Prem Naam Hai Mera, Prem Chopra.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>'Prem Naam Hai Mera, Prem Chopra' (a title playing on his famous dialogue) is the autobiography of veteran Bollywood actor Prem Chopra, chronicling his long career as one of Hindi cinema's most memorable villains.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Books &amp; Authors (autobiographies of notable personalities) is a regular SSC static GK topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30042","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>In the mega consolidation in public sector banks in 2020, which of the following banks were merged into Union Bank of India?</span></p>","option_1":"Oriental Bank of Commerce and United Bank of India","option_2":"Syndicate Bank","option_3":"Andhra Bank and Corporation Bank","option_4":"Allahabad Bank","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Andhra Bank and Corporation Bank.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>As part of the mega bank merger announced in 2019 and effective from 1 April 2020, Andhra Bank and Corporation Bank were merged into Union Bank of India.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Oriental Bank of Commerce and United Bank of India were merged into Punjab National Bank, Syndicate Bank was merged into Canara Bank, and Allahabad Bank was merged into Indian Bank - these were separate mergers in the same consolidation exercise. Bank mergers and consolidation of PSBs are a frequently tested SSC Economy/Banking topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30043","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>In competition of wrestling, the refereeing for each bout shall consist of which of the following?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>I. Mat Chairman</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>II. Referee</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>As per international wrestling rules (UWW), the officiating team for each bout consists of three officials: the Referee (on the mat), the Judge, and the Mat Chairman, who together score and adjudicate the bout.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Mat Chairman has the authority to overrule decisions in case of disagreement. Hence both the Mat Chairman and Referee are part of the officiating panel, making both statements correct. Sports rules and officiating structures are a recurring SSC topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30044","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Hawa Mahal is dedicated to which of the following deity?</span></p>","option_1":"Lord Krishna","option_2":"Lord Indra","option_3":"Lord Buddha","option_4":"Lord Shiva","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Lord Krishna.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Hawa Mahal ('Palace of Winds') in Jaipur, built in 1799 by Maharaja Sawai Pratap Singh, is designed in the shape of Lord Krishna's crown (mukut), as the Maharaja was a devotee of Krishna.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Its intricate honeycomb facade with 953 small windows (jharokhas) was built to allow royal ladies to observe street festivities while maintaining purdah. Rajasthani monuments and their architectural/religious significance are a common SSC Art &amp; Culture topic.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30045","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"786","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Who was the first recipient of the Dadasaheb Phalke Award in 1969?</span></p>","option_1":"Ashapoorna Devi","option_2":"Devika Rani","option_3":"Madhubala","option_4":"Bindu","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Devika Rani.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Devika Rani, a pioneering actress often referred to as the 'First Lady of Indian Cinema', was the first recipient of the Dadasaheb Phalke Award, India's highest award in cinema, instituted in 1969.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>This is a landmark fact in Indian film history frequently tested in SSC General Awareness.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]